Q10. Rebuilt a Denormalized result with join\
\
SELECT\
    o.order_id,\
    o.order_date,\
    c.customer_name,\
    c.customer_city,\
    p.product_name,\
    oi.quantity,\
    oi.unit_price_gbp,\
    (oi.quantity * oi.unit_price_gbp) AS line_total_gbp\
FROM orders o\
JOIN customers   c  ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\
JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id   = o.order_id\
JOIN products    p  ON p.product_id  = oi.product_id\
ORDER BY o.order_id, p.product_name;\
\
Q.11 compare update behavior\
\
products\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf10 Model A needs fewer changes. The product name is stored once, in 
\f3\fs28 products
\f1\fs32 , so a rename is a single 
\f3\fs28 UPDATE
\f1\fs32  regardless of how many sales exist. Model B stores a copy in every order line, so the same rename requires one update per line ever sold \'97 the cost grows with sales volume.\
The reason is redundancy. In Model A the name is a fact about the product, and 
\f3\fs28 order_items
\f1\fs32  holds only a reference (
\f3\fs28 product_id
\f1\fs32 ), which doesn't need to change. In Model B each line holds an independent copy of the fact, so every copy must be updated separately \'97 and if any update is missed, the database becomes inconsistent (the same product with two different names), which is the classic update anomaly that 3NF eliminates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf10  Model B preserves the name as it was at the time of sale, which matters for invoices. The best design keeps the name in 
\f3\fs28 products
\f1\fs32  for the catalogue and a snapshot column in 
\f3\fs28 order_items
\f1\fs32  (e.g. 
\f3\fs28 product_name_at_sale
\f1\fs32 ) so updates stay cheap and history stays accurate.\
\
